# SYNOPSIS

```usage
expr arg ...
```

# DESCRIPTION

`expr` evaluates the expression its arguments make, prints the result and a newline, and exits by whether the result is null or zero.
Each operator and operand is a separate argument, and the shell's special characters among them must be quoted.

The operators, each between operands <a> and <b>, from the loosest binding to the tightest; those on one row bind equally and group from the left.
Parentheses, `(` and `)`, group.

| Operators | Value |
| `|` | <a> if it is neither null nor zero, else <b> |
| `&` | <a> if neither is null or zero, else 0 |
| `=` `!=` `<` `<=` `>` `>=` | 1 if the comparison holds, else 0 |
| `+` `-` | the sum or difference |
| `*` `/` `%` | the product, quotient or remainder |
| `:` | the match of a regular expression, below |

A comparison is of numbers if both operands are decimal integers, and otherwise of strings, byte by byte.
The arithmetic operators take decimal integers, optionally signed, that fit 64 bits; the quotient is truncated toward zero.

`:` matches <a> against the basic regular expression <b>, as musl's `regcomp` compiles it, anchored at the start of <a> as if <b> began with `^`.
If <b> holds a subexpression, `\(` ... `\)`, the value is the part of <a> the first one matched, or the null string when <a> does not match.
Otherwise the value is the number of characters matched, counting a UTF-8 sequence as one, or 0.

`expr` is sbase's, and found only in the POSIX namespace: `/boot/bin/posix/expr` is a hard link to `sbase-box`, which runs the command its name names, and `/lib/ns/posix` (a manifest's `ns=posix`, see namespace(6)) binds `/boot/bin/posix` before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program that runs `expr` finds it there.
There is no native `expr`.

# EXAMPLES

`tests/posix/sbasetest.c` runs `expr 3 + 4` in the POSIX namespace and checks that it prints `7` and exits 0.

